-- title:
--   Ends of the blow-up on components through the supersingular point
-- statement:
--   Notation. For a commutative ring $R$, a field $F$ with $R$-algebra structure and $j \in F$, `chartAlgFin R F j` is the $R$-subalgebra of $F$ consisting of the elements integral over $R[j]$, `jChartFin` is $j$ regarded as an element of it, `XFin` is its spectrum, and `TwoChartIntegralModel R F j` is the scheme obtained as the pushout of the two maps from `XMid` to `XFin` and to the spectrum of `chartAlgInf R F j` (the analogous algebra attached to $j^{-1}$), with `toBase` the structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} R$ and `ιFin` the canonical morphism from `XFin`. In what follows $C :=$ `chartAlgFin A ↥K j`, $\mathcal X :=$ `TwoChartIntegralModel A ↥K j`, and for a point $z$ of $\mathcal X$, `germY` denotes the ring homomorphism $C \to \mathcal O_{\mathcal X, z}$ obtained from the inverse of the global-sections isomorphism for $\operatorname{Spec} C$, the isomorphism induced by `ιFin` on the top open, and the germ map at $z$; `toC` denotes the map from the stalk to its adic completion with respect to the maximal ideal. Finally, `drinfeldForm q O` is $X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1 \in O[[X_0,X_1]]$, `ssJSet q Ω` is the set of those $j \in \Omega$ such that every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $j$ has no non-zero affine point killed by $q$, `CoordRing q k` is $k[X_0,X_1]/(\mathrm{drinfeldPoly}\, q\,k - 1)$, `hSubgroup q` is the kernel of the character $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb Z/q) \times \mathbb F_{q^2}^\times \to \mathbb F_{q^2}^\times$, $(g,c) \mapsto \det(g)\, c^{q+1}$, with `hAction` its action by $k$-algebra automorphisms on `CoordRing q k`, and `redQ q γ` is the image of $γ \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb Z/q)$.
--
--   Data and standing hypotheses. Let $q$ be a prime, $M'$ a non-zero natural number with $q \nmid M'$ (`hqM'`), and let $\ell$ be a prime with $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ (`hℓ12`) and $\ell \mid M'$ (`hℓM'`). Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero, $ζ \in L$ a primitive $q$-th root of unity (`hζ`), and assume (`hι`) that there is a ring homomorphism $ι : L \to \mathbb C$ with $ι(ζ) = e^{2\pi i/q}$. Let $H_1 \le (\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ be (`hH₁`) the intersection of `levelH q M'`, the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/q)^\times$ (the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$), with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/\ell)^\times$. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq L((t))$ equal (`hK`) to the field generated over $L$ by the image under coefficientwise base change of the $q$-expansion function field `xHFunctionField (q^2*M') H₁` of $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$. Let $A$ be a henselian discrete valuation domain with algebraically closed residue field, an $A$-algebra structure on $L$ making $L$ the fraction field of $A$, together with compatible $A$-algebra and scalar-tower structures on $K$; assume $q$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ (`hAq`) and $ζ$ lies in the image of $A$ (`hζA`). Let $j \in K$, non-zero, whose Laurent series is the image of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) of the $j$-function under coefficientwise base change (`hj`). Let $ϖ \in A$ generate the maximal ideal (`hϖ`), and let $ϖ_t \in A$ satisfy $ϖ_t^{q^2-1} = q\,u$ for some unit $u$ (`hϖt`).
--
--   Let $y$ be a maximal ideal of $C$ (`hy`) containing $ϖ$ (`hϖy`) and supersingular in the sense (`hss`) that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $φ : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$ one has $φ(j) \in$ `ssJSet q Ω`.
--
--   The Drinfeld chart clause list. For a point $z$ of $\mathcal X$ at which the germ $ϖ_z$ of $ϖ$ (pulled back through `toBase`) lies in the maximal ideal of $\mathcal O_{\mathcal X,z}$, for a point $y'$ of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ with `ιFin`$(y') = z$ satisfying the same supersingularity condition as `hss` for $y'$, and for data consisting of a complete discrete valuation domain $W$, a ring homomorphism $σ : A \to W$ with maximal ideal generated by $σ(ϖ)$, power series $f, u, v \in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u, v$ units and $f \equiv$ `drinfeldForm q W` modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and a ring isomorphism $e$ from the adic completion of $\mathcal O_{\mathcal X,z}$ onto
--   $$S := W[[X_0,X_1]]/\bigl(C(σ(ϖ_t^{q+1}))\,v - f\,u \bigr),$$
--   with $\mathrm{mk}_S$ the quotient map, the clause list in question is the conjunction of the following seven statements.
--
--   (i) For every $a \in A$, $e$ carries the germ at $z$ of $a$ (through `toBase`, completed) to the class of the constant series $C(σ a)$.
--
--   (ii) For every $γ \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and every $L$-algebra automorphism $τ$ of $K$ with `IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q^2*M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ` — that is, $τ$ realises on quotients of integral $q$-expansions of modular forms for $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ the substitution by the matrix `conjElemN q γ⁻¹`, after transport to $\mathbb C$ along any $ι$ with $ι(ζ) = e^{2\pi i /q}$ — the automorphism $τ$ maps $C$ into $C$.
--
--   (iii) For every such $γ$ with in addition $γ_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod \ell$ and every such $τ$ preserving $C$, the induced endomorphism of $C$ satisfies $τ(a) - a \in y'$ for all $a \in C$.
--
--   (iv) For every such $γ$ and $τ$ preserving $C$ and satisfying $τ(a) - a \in y'$ for all $a \in C$, there are a ring automorphism $θ$ of $S$, an element $c \in W$ and a matrix $M \in M_2(W)$ such that: $θ$ is compatible with $τ$ through $e \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY}$; $θ$ fixes the classes of all constant series; $θ(\mathrm{mk}_S X_j) - \mathrm{mk}_S\bigl(\sum_i C(M_{ij})X_i\bigr)$ lies in the square of the ideal generated by the classes of $X_0, X_1$; $c^{q+1} \equiv 1$ modulo the maximal ideal of $W$; $M_{ij} \equiv c\,γ_{ij}$ modulo the maximal ideal of $W$ for all $i,j$; if $γ_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod \ell$ then $c \equiv 1$ modulo the maximal ideal of $W$; and if $γ \in \Gamma(q)$ and $τ$ is not the identity then $c - 1$ is a unit.
--
--   (v) For all integers $a_1,b_1,a_2,b_2$ and all primes $P_1, P_2$ of $S$ such that each $P_i$ omits at least one of the classes of $X_0, X_1$, contains the class of $C(σ ϖ)$, and contains an element of the form $\mathrm{mk}_S(C(a_i)X_0 + C(b_i)X_1 + h)$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, if $q \nmid a_1b_2 - a_2b_1$ then the contractions of $P_1$ and $P_2$ to $\mathcal O_{\mathcal X,z}$ along $e \circ \mathrm{toC}$ are distinct.
--
--   (vi) For every prime $P$ of $S$ omitting at least one of the classes of $X_0, X_1$, containing the class of $C(σϖ)$ and containing an element $\mathrm{mk}_S(C(1)X_0 + C(0)X_1 + h)$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, and for every $a \in C$: the germ of $a$ lies in the contraction of $P$ along $e$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image in $L$ of the maximal ideal of $A$.
--
--   (vii) The element `jqNModC L q` (the $q$-fold $q$-expansion rescaling of the $j$-series) lies in $K$ and in $C$; there are $a_0 \in A$ with the difference of this element of $C$ and $a_0$ lying in $y'$, an integer $e_0 \ge 1$ and $h \in (X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ such that for all $a, b \in W$, at least one of which is a unit, with $a^qb - ab^q$ in the maximal ideal of $W$, the value $\sum_{i=0}^{e_0} \mathrm{coeff}_{X_0^iX_1^{e_0-i}}(h)\,a^i b^{e_0-i}$ of the degree-$e_0$ leading form of $h$ is a unit; and $e$ carries the germ of that difference to $\mathrm{mk}_S h$.
--
--   Remaining hypotheses. The hypothesis `hArig` asserts that for every $z$, $ϖ_z$, $y'$ as above (with the supersingularity condition at $y'$) there exist $W, σ, f, u, v, e$ as above for which the clause list (i)–(vii) holds. Further data are fixed: a point $z$ of $\mathcal X$ with germ $ϖ_z$ of $ϖ$ in the maximal ideal of the stalk (`hϖz`, `hz`), a point $y'$ of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ with `ιFin`$(y') = z$ (`hy'`), supersingular in the above sense (`hss'`), with $y' = y$ (`hy'y`); and a chart witness $W_1, σ_1, f_1, u_1, v_1, e_1$ (with `hσ₁`, `hu₁`, `hv₁`, `hf₁`) for which the clause list (i)–(vii) holds, this being the hypothesis `hW₁`, whose seven conjuncts are exactly those listed above for $S = W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/\bigl(C(σ_1(ϖ_t^{q+1}))v_1 - f_1u_1\bigr)$.
--
--   Next, $J$ is an ideal of $C$ equal (`hJ`) to the infimum of the set of those ideals $J'$ for which there exist $γ \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and an $L$-algebra automorphism $τ$ of $K$ with `IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q^2*M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ`, preserving $C$, such that $J'$ is the contraction along the induced endomorphism of $C$ of the contraction along $e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY}$ of the ideal of $S$ generated by the classes of $C(σ_1ϖ_t)$, $X_0$ and $X_1$.
--
--   Let $B$ be the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ equal (`hB`) to the $C$-algebra generated by $\{x \in K \mid \exists i \in J,\ x\,ϖ_t = i\}$, i.e. $C[J/ϖ_t]$, and let $W$ be a valuation subring of $K$ containing $B$ (`hBW`). The hypotheses on this pair are grouped as follows. `hR1`: $C \subseteq B$, and every $f \in K$ is a quotient $g/h$ with $g, h \in B$, $h \neq 0$. `hR2`: $B$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A$, and $B/ϖB$ has Krull dimension at most $1$. `hR3` (five clauses): an element of $L$ lies in $W$ exactly when it comes from $A$; the maximal ideal of $W$ is generated by $ϖ$; $W$ is a discrete valuation ring; an element of $C$ lies in $y$ exactly when it lies in $W$ and in the maximal ideal of $W$; and $f \in K$ lies in $W$ exactly when $f = g/h$ with $g, h \in B$ and $h$ a unit of $W$ (so $W$ is the localisation of $B$ at the centre determined by $y$).
--
--   The hypothesis `hEQ` has five conjuncts. First, for every $\mathbb F_{q^2}$-algebra structure on the residue field of $A$ there is a surjective ring homomorphism $ρ$ from $B$ onto [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q (ResidueField A)`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) whose kernel consists of the elements of $B$ lying in the maximal ideal of $W$, which is compatible with $A$ via the residue map, and which is equivariant in the following sense: for every $γ \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and every level automorphism $τ$ at $γ^{-1}$ in the above sense which preserves $W$ (i.e. $f \in W \iff τ f \in W$), there is $c \in \mathbb F_{q^2}^\times$ with $(\mathrm{redQ}\, q\,γ, c) \in$ `hSubgroup q` such that $ρ(τ b) =$ `hAction`$(\,(\mathrm{redQ}\, q\,γ,c)\,)(ρ b)$ for all $b \in B$ with $τ b \in B$, and such that $c \neq 1$ whenever $γ \in \Gamma(q)$ and $τ$ is not the identity. Second, $B$ is stable under every level automorphism at $γ^{-1}$ for $γ \in \Gamma_0(M')$. Third, for every prime $Q$ of $B$ containing $ϖ$ there are $γ \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and a level automorphism $τ$ at $γ^{-1}$ carrying every $b \in B$ lying in the maximal ideal of $W$ into $B$ and into $Q$. Fourth, an element $b \in B$ is divisible by $ϖ$ in $B$ as soon as, for all $γ \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and all level automorphisms $τ$ at $γ^{-1}$ with $τ b \in B$, the element $τ b$ lies in the maximal ideal of $W$. Fifth, for $γ \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and $τ$ a level automorphism at $γ^{-1}$: if $τ$ preserves $y$ on $C$ (for $b \in C$ with $τ b \in C$, $b \in y \iff τ b \in y$), then $f \in W \iff τ f \in W$ for all $f \in K$.
--
--   Conclusion. Under these hypotheses, for every ideal $\mathfrak p$ of $C$ which is prime, contains $ϖ$, is contained in $y$ and differs from $y$, there is a subring $O$ of $K$ with the following two properties.
--
--   First, there are an element $a \in C$ lying in $J$ and non-zero in $K$ and, with $B_a$ denoting the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ generated over $C$ by $\{x \in K \mid \exists i \in J,\ x\,a = i\}$ (that is, $C[J/a]$), a maximal ideal $P$ of $B_a$ such that: an element $f \in K$ lies in $O$ precisely when $f = g/h$ with $g, h \in B_a$ and $h \notin P$ (so $O$ is the localisation of $B_a$ at $P$); every $b \in y$ which lies in $O$ is a non-unit of $O$; and $B$ is not contained in $O$.
--
--   Second, there is a prime ideal $\mathfrak q$ of $O$ such that: if $ϖ$ lies in $O$, then it lies in $\mathfrak q$; some $b \in C$ belonging to $y$ fails to lie in $\mathfrak q$ whenever it lies in $O$; and for every $c \in C$ lying in $O$, $c \in \mathfrak p$ if and only if $c \in \mathfrak q$.
--
--   This is a step in the analysis of the supersingular part of the special fibre of the integral model, in the auxiliary frame where the level is cut out by the subgroup $H_1$ of units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$ and modulo a guard prime $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ dividing $M'$, so that the condition '$γ$ trivial at the auxiliary level' is replaced by '$γ_{11} \equiv 1 \bmod \ell$'. It produces, on each component of the special fibre through the supersingular point $y$, a local ring of the blow-up of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ along the weighted centre $J$ that is distinct from the Drinfeld-curve chart $B = C[J/ϖ_t]$, and is used by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevelOne.blowupChart_primes_over_supersingular_exceptional_generic_or_end_on_unique_component_of_drinfeldChartWitness_linked_of_dvd`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevelOne.blowupChart_primes_over_supersingular_exceptional_generic_or_end_on_unique_component_of_drinfeldChartWitness_linked_of_dvd).
